window.__topicData({"cat":"living","topics":[{"id":"faith","name":"Faith","overview":"Faith in the Bible is trust in God and His promises, even when outcomes are not yet visible. Hebrews 11 gives a 'hall of faith' of people who acted on that trust, and the New Testament presents faith as the way a person receives God's grace and lives it out.","refs":[{"ref":"Hebrews 11","note":"Definition of faith and examples from Abel to the prophets"},{"ref":"Genesis 15:1–6","note":"Abram believed God, and it was counted as righteousness"},{"ref":"Romans 4","note":"Paul explains Abraham's faith"},{"ref":"Romans 10:17","note":"Faith comes by hearing the word"},{"ref":"James 2:14–26","note":"Faith shown through works"},{"ref":"Mark 9:14–29","note":"'Lord, I believe; help thou mine unbelief'"},{"ref":"Matthew 17:20","note":"Faith as a grain of mustard seed"}],"terms":[{"label":"Faith","value":"Confident trust in God and His word (Hebrews 11:1)."},{"label":"Belief","value":"Accepting something as true; in Scripture, often personal trust."},{"label":"Righteousness","value":"Right standing with God; credited to Abraham through faith."},{"label":"Works","value":"Actions that show faith is alive (James 2)."},{"label":"Doubt","value":"Uncertainty; Scripture shows God meeting people in it (Mark 9:24)."}],"questions":["In your own words, how does Hebrews 11:1 define faith?","Which person in Hebrews 11 do you relate to most, and why?","How do Romans 4 and James 2 fit together on faith and works?","What helps your faith grow, according to Romans 10:17 and your own experience?","How does the father's prayer in Mark 9:24 speak to times of doubt?","What is one step of trust you could take this week?"],"quiz":[{"q":"Which chapter is often called the 'hall of faith'?","a":"Hebrews 11."},{"q":"According to Hebrews 11:6, without faith it is impossible to do what?","a":"To please God."},{"q":"Whose faith was 'counted unto him for righteousness' in Genesis 15:6?","a":"Abram (Abraham)."},{"q":"James says faith without works is what?","a":"Dead (James 2:17, 26)."},{"q":"Jesus compared small faith to what seed?","a":"A grain of mustard seed (Matthew 17:20)."}],"verse":{"text":"Now faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen.","ref":"Hebrews 11:1 (KJV)"},"cat":"living"},{"id":"prayer","name":"Prayer","overview":"Prayer is talking with God: praise, thanks, confession, and requests. Jesus taught His disciples how to pray and modeled a life of prayer, and the Psalms give words for every emotion. Scripture invites believers to pray honestly, persistently, and with trust.","refs":[{"ref":"Matthew 6:5–15","note":"Jesus teaches the Lord's Prayer"},{"ref":"Luke 11:1–13","note":"Ask, seek, knock"},{"ref":"Luke 18:1–8","note":"The persistent widow"},{"ref":"Philippians 4:6–7","note":"Prayer with thanksgiving and the peace of God"},{"ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:16–18","note":"Pray without ceasing"},{"ref":"Psalm 51","note":"David's prayer of confession"},{"ref":"Mark 14:32–36","note":"Jesus prays in Gethsemane"},{"ref":"James 5:13–16","note":"Prayer for the sick and for one another"}],"terms":[{"label":"Supplication","value":"Humble, earnest request."},{"label":"Intercession","value":"Praying on behalf of others."},{"label":"Confession","value":"Admitting sin to God (Psalm 51; 1 John 1:9)."},{"label":"Thanksgiving","value":"Gratitude expressed to God."},{"label":"Amen","value":"Hebrew word meaning 'so be it' or 'truly'."}],"questions":["Which parts of the Lord's Prayer focus on God, and which on our needs?","What do you think 'pray without ceasing' looks like in a busy day?","What does Jesus' prayer in Gethsemane teach about honest prayer?","Why might Jesus tell a parable about persistence in prayer?","How does Philippians 4:6–7 connect prayer and anxiety?","Which kind of prayer (praise, thanks, confession, request) comes least naturally to you?"],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Matthew record the Lord's Prayer?","a":"Matthew 6 (verses 9–13)."},{"q":"In Luke 11, what three verbs does Jesus use about prayer?","a":"Ask, seek, knock (Luke 11:9)."},{"q":"Where did Jesus pray before His arrest?","a":"Gethsemane (Mark 14:32)."},{"q":"Which psalm is David's prayer after his sin with Bathsheba?","a":"Psalm 51."},{"q":"According to Philippians 4:7, what will keep your heart and mind?","a":"The peace of God, which passeth all understanding."}],"verse":{"text":"Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God.","ref":"Philippians 4:6 (KJV)"},"cat":"living"},{"id":"forgiveness","name":"Forgiveness","overview":"Forgiveness runs in two directions in Scripture: God forgives those who turn to Him, and those who are forgiven are called to forgive others. Jesus taught about forgiveness often and forgave even from the cross. Forgiveness releases a debt; it is not the same as pretending harm did not happen.","refs":[{"ref":"Matthew 18:21–35","note":"Seventy times seven; the unforgiving servant"},{"ref":"Matthew 6:12, 14–15","note":"Forgive us as we forgive"},{"ref":"1 John 1:9","note":"If we confess, He is faithful to forgive"},{"ref":"Psalm 103:8–12","note":"As far as the east is from the west"},{"ref":"Luke 23:34","note":"'Father, forgive them'"},{"ref":"Genesis 45 and 50:15–21","note":"Joseph forgives his brothers"},{"ref":"Colossians 3:12–14","note":"Forgive as the Lord forgave you"}],"terms":[{"label":"Forgiveness","value":"Releasing someone from a debt or wrong."},{"label":"Repentance","value":"Turning away from sin and toward God."},{"label":"Reconciliation","value":"Restoring a broken relationship."},{"label":"Trespass","value":"A wrong or offense (Matthew 6:14)."},{"label":"Atonement","value":"God's provision for covering sin and restoring relationship."}],"questions":["What point is Jesus making with the number 'seventy times seven'?","How does the parable of the unforgiving servant connect God's forgiveness with ours?","What stands out in how Joseph spoke to his brothers in Genesis 50:20?","How is forgiveness different from trust or reconciliation?","What does Psalm 103:12 say about how God deals with forgiven sin?","What makes forgiving hard, and what helps?"],"quiz":[{"q":"Who asked Jesus how many times he should forgive?","a":"Peter (Matthew 18:21)."},{"q":"What did Jesus answer?","a":"Until seventy times seven (Matthew 18:22)."},{"q":"What did Jesus say about those crucifying Him in Luke 23:34?","a":"'Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do.'"},{"q":"Who said 'ye thought evil against me; but God meant it unto good'?","a":"Joseph (Genesis 50:20)."},{"q":"According to 1 John 1:9, what must we do to be forgiven?","a":"Confess our sins."}],"verse":{"text":"And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ's sake hath forgiven you.","ref":"Ephesians 4:32 (KJV)"},"cat":"living"},{"id":"humility","name":"Humility","cat":"living","overview":"Humility is an honest view of ourselves before God: recognizing our dependence on Him and putting others ahead of ourselves. Scripture warns that pride goes before destruction and promises that God gives grace to the humble. Jesus is the supreme example, washing His disciples' feet and humbling Himself to the cross.","refs":[{"ref":"Proverbs 16:18–19","note":"Pride goeth before destruction"},{"ref":"Micah 6:8","note":"Walk humbly with thy God"},{"ref":"Matthew 18:1–4","note":"Become as little children"},{"ref":"Luke 14:7–11","note":"Take the lowest seat"},{"ref":"John 13:1–17","note":"Jesus washes the disciples' feet"},{"ref":"Philippians 2:3–8","note":"In lowliness of mind let each esteem other better than themselves"},{"ref":"James 4:6–10","note":"God resisteth the proud, but giveth grace unto the humble"}],"terms":[{"label":"Humility","value":"A lowly, honest estimate of oneself before God and others."},{"label":"Pride","value":"Self-exaltation and reliance on oneself instead of God."},{"label":"Lowliness","value":"KJV word for humility (Ephesians 4:2)."},{"label":"Servanthood","value":"Choosing to serve others as Jesus did."},{"label":"Exalt","value":"To lift up or honor."}],"questions":["Why do you think pride is so dangerous in Scripture?","What did Jesus teach by washing the disciples' feet?","How does Luke 14:11 turn the world's idea of honor upside down?","What would it look like to \"esteem others better than yourself\" this week?","How is humility different from low self-worth?"],"quiz":[{"q":"According to Proverbs 16:18, what goes before destruction?","a":"Pride."},{"q":"Whom did Jesus set in the midst of the disciples as an example of humility?","a":"A little child (Matthew 18:2)."},{"q":"According to James 4:6, to whom does God give grace?","a":"The humble."},{"q":"What did Jesus do with a towel and basin in John 13?","a":"Washed His disciples' feet."},{"q":"In Luke 14, where did Jesus say to sit at a feast?","a":"In the lowest room (lowest place)."}],"verse":{"text":"Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord, and he shall lift you up.","ref":"James 4:10 (KJV)"}},{"id":"obedience","name":"Obedience","cat":"living","overview":"Biblical obedience is hearing God's word and doing it out of love and trust. Jesus said that those who love Him keep His commandments, and He Himself learned obedience through suffering. Obedience is not a way to earn God's favor but a response to His grace.","refs":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 10:12–13","note":"What doth the Lord require of thee?"},{"ref":"1 Samuel 15:22–23","note":"To obey is better than sacrifice"},{"ref":"John 14:15, 21–24","note":"If ye love me, keep my commandments"},{"ref":"James 1:22–25","note":"Be doers of the word, not hearers only"},{"ref":"Hebrews 5:7–9","note":"Though a Son, yet learned He obedience"},{"ref":"Acts 5:27–32","note":"We ought to obey God rather than men"},{"ref":"Luke 6:46–49","note":"Why call ye me Lord, and do not the things which I say?"}],"terms":[{"label":"Obedience","value":"Willingly doing what God commands."},{"label":"Commandment","value":"A direct instruction from God."},{"label":"Hearer","value":"One who listens without acting (James 1:23)."},{"label":"Doer","value":"One who puts God's word into practice."},{"label":"Submission","value":"Yielding one's will to God."}],"questions":["Why did Samuel tell Saul that obedience is better than sacrifice?","How does Jesus connect love and obedience in John 14?","What is the danger of being a hearer only, according to James?","When might obeying God mean disobeying human authorities, as in Acts 5?","What is one area where God is calling you to obey now?"],"quiz":[{"q":"Which prophet told Saul \"to obey is better than sacrifice\"?","a":"Samuel (1 Samuel 15:22)."},{"q":"According to John 14:15, how do we show love for Jesus?","a":"By keeping His commandments."},{"q":"James compares a hearer only to someone looking at what?","a":"His natural face in a glass (mirror) (James 1:23)."},{"q":"What did Peter and the apostles answer the council in Acts 5:29?","a":"\"We ought to obey God rather than men.\""},{"q":"According to Hebrews 5:8, how did Jesus learn obedience?","a":"By the things which He suffered."}],"verse":{"text":"If ye love me, keep my commandments.","ref":"John 14:15 (KJV)"}},{"id":"wisdom","name":"Wisdom","cat":"living","overview":"Biblical wisdom is skill in living rightly before God. It begins with the fear of the Lord, is found especially in Proverbs, Job, and Ecclesiastes, and is offered freely to those who ask. The New Testament presents Christ as the wisdom of God and contrasts earthly wisdom with the wisdom from above.","refs":[{"ref":"Proverbs 1:1–7","note":"The fear of the Lord is the beginning of knowledge"},{"ref":"Proverbs 2:1–9","note":"Search for wisdom as for hidden treasure"},{"ref":"Proverbs 3:5–7","note":"Trust in the Lord with all thine heart"},{"ref":"1 Kings 3:5–14","note":"Solomon asks for an understanding heart"},{"ref":"James 1:5–8","note":"If any lack wisdom, let him ask of God"},{"ref":"James 3:13–18","note":"The wisdom that is from above"},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 1:22–31","note":"Christ the power and wisdom of God"}],"terms":[{"label":"Wisdom","value":"Skill in living rightly, grounded in reverence for God."},{"label":"Fear of the Lord","value":"Reverent awe and submission toward God."},{"label":"Folly","value":"Moral foolishness that ignores God."},{"label":"Understanding","value":"Insight to discern what is true and right."},{"label":"Prudence","value":"Practical good judgment."}],"questions":["Why does Proverbs say the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom?","What did Solomon ask for, and why did it please God?","How does James 3 describe wisdom from above?","Where do you need God's wisdom for a decision right now?","How is biblical wisdom different from intelligence or cleverness?"],"quiz":[{"q":"According to Proverbs 1:7, what is the beginning of knowledge?","a":"The fear of the Lord."},{"q":"What did Solomon ask God for?","a":"An understanding heart to judge God's people (1 Kings 3:9)."},{"q":"According to James 1:5, how does God give wisdom?","a":"Liberally (generously), without upbraiding."},{"q":"Complete Proverbs 3:5: \"Trust in the Lord with all thine heart…\"","a":"\"…and lean not unto thine own understanding.\""},{"q":"Name one quality of wisdom from above in James 3:17.","a":"Pure, peaceable, gentle, easy to be intreated, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality or hypocrisy."}],"verse":{"text":"If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God, that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him.","ref":"James 1:5 (KJV)"}},{"id":"contentment","name":"Contentment","cat":"living","overview":"Contentment is a settled peace with what God has provided, whatever our circumstances. Paul learned to be content in plenty and in want, and Scripture warns that the love of money and covetousness lead people astray. Contentment rests in God's presence rather than possessions.","refs":[{"ref":"Philippians 4:10–13","note":"I have learned to be content"},{"ref":"1 Timothy 6:6–10","note":"Godliness with contentment is great gain"},{"ref":"Hebrews 13:5–6","note":"I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee"},{"ref":"Exodus 20:17","note":"Thou shalt not covet"},{"ref":"Proverbs 30:7–9","note":"Give me neither poverty nor riches"},{"ref":"Psalm 23","note":"The Lord is my shepherd; I shall not want"},{"ref":"Luke 12:13–21","note":"Beware of covetousness"}],"terms":[{"label":"Contentment","value":"Inner satisfaction and peace regardless of circumstances."},{"label":"Covetousness","value":"Craving what belongs to others."},{"label":"Godliness","value":"A life of devotion and reverence toward God."},{"label":"Gain","value":"Profit or advantage."},{"label":"Sufficiency","value":"Having enough (2 Corinthians 9:8)."}],"questions":["What did Paul say was the secret of his contentment?","Why does Hebrews 13:5 connect contentment with God's presence?","What is wise about the prayer in Proverbs 30:8–9?","What triggers discontent in your life, and how can you respond?","How does Psalm 23 picture a contented life?"],"quiz":[{"q":"According to 1 Timothy 6:10, what is the root of all evil?","a":"The love of money."},{"q":"Which commandment forbids coveting?","a":"The tenth commandment (Exodus 20:17)."},{"q":"Complete Philippians 4:13: \"I can do all things…\"","a":"\"…through Christ which strengtheneth me.\""},{"q":"According to 1 Timothy 6:6, godliness with contentment is what?","a":"Great gain."},{"q":"What promise does Hebrews 13:5 quote?","a":"\"I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee.\""}],"verse":{"text":"Let your conversation be without covetousness; and be content with such things as ye have: for he hath said, I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee.","ref":"Hebrews 13:5 (KJV)"}},{"id":"generosity","name":"Generosity","cat":"living","overview":"Generosity reflects the generous heart of God, who gave His Son. Scripture calls believers to give freely, cheerfully, and without show, especially to the poor and those in need. Generous giving is less about the amount and more about the heart, as the widow's two mites show.","refs":[{"ref":"2 Corinthians 9:6–11","note":"God loveth a cheerful giver"},{"ref":"2 Corinthians 8:1–9","note":"The Macedonians gave beyond their power"},{"ref":"Mark 12:41–44","note":"The widow's two mites"},{"ref":"Acts 20:35","note":"It is more blessed to give than to receive"},{"ref":"Proverbs 11:24–25","note":"The liberal soul shall be made fat"},{"ref":"Luke 6:38","note":"Give, and it shall be given unto you"},{"ref":"1 John 3:16–18","note":"Love in deed and in truth"}],"terms":[{"label":"Generosity","value":"Freely giving time, money, and help to others."},{"label":"Liberal","value":"In the KJV, generous (Proverbs 11:25)."},{"label":"Mite","value":"A very small coin."},{"label":"Alms","value":"Gifts to the poor."},{"label":"Cheerful","value":"Glad and willing (Greek \"hilaros\")."}],"questions":["Why did Jesus praise the widow who gave two mites?","What does 2 Corinthians 8:9 say about Christ's generosity?","Why does God love a cheerful giver?","How does 1 John 3:17 link generosity with love?","What is one generous act you could do this week?"],"quiz":[{"q":"According to 2 Corinthians 9:7, whom does God love?","a":"A cheerful giver."},{"q":"How much did the poor widow give?","a":"Two mites (Mark 12:42)."},{"q":"Whose words did Paul quote: \"It is more blessed to give than to receive\"?","a":"The Lord Jesus (Acts 20:35)."},{"q":"Which churches gave generously out of deep poverty in 2 Corinthians 8?","a":"The churches of Macedonia."},{"q":"According to Luke 6:38, with what measure will it be measured to you?","a":"The same measure you use."}],"verse":{"text":"Every man according as he purposeth in his heart, so let him give; not grudgingly, or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver.","ref":"2 Corinthians 9:7 (KJV)"}},{"id":"stewardship","name":"Stewardship","cat":"living","overview":"A steward manages what belongs to someone else. Scripture teaches that everything belongs to God, and people are entrusted with creation, time, talents, money, and the gospel itself. Faithful stewards use these gifts for God's purposes and will give account to Him.","refs":[{"ref":"Psalm 24:1","note":"The earth is the Lord's, and the fulness thereof"},{"ref":"Genesis 2:15","note":"Put in the garden to dress it and keep it"},{"ref":"Matthew 25:14–30","note":"The parable of the talents"},{"ref":"Luke 16:10–13","note":"Faithful in that which is least"},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 4:1–2","note":"It is required in stewards that a man be found faithful"},{"ref":"1 Peter 4:10–11","note":"Good stewards of the manifold grace of God"},{"ref":"Ephesians 5:15–16","note":"Redeeming the time"}],"terms":[{"label":"Steward","value":"A manager entrusted with another's property."},{"label":"Talent","value":"A large sum of money; also used today for abilities."},{"label":"Accountability","value":"Having to give an account for what was entrusted."},{"label":"Redeeming the time","value":"Making the most of every opportunity."},{"label":"Manifold","value":"Many and varied."}],"questions":["What does Psalm 24:1 imply about ownership?","Why was the one-talent servant judged in Matthew 25?","What does Luke 16:10 teach about small responsibilities?","Which of your gifts or resources is God asking you to steward better?","How does Genesis 2:15 shape a Christian view of caring for creation?"],"quiz":[{"q":"According to Psalm 24:1, to whom does the earth belong?","a":"The Lord."},{"q":"What is required of stewards in 1 Corinthians 4:2?","a":"That they be found faithful."},{"q":"What did the one-talent servant do with his talent?","a":"Hid it in the earth (Matthew 25:25)."},{"q":"Where did God put Adam to dress and keep it?","a":"The garden of Eden (Genesis 2:15)."},{"q":"According to 1 Peter 4:10, what are believers to be good stewards of?","a":"The manifold grace of God."}],"verse":{"text":"As every man hath received the gift, even so minister the same one to another, as good stewards of the manifold grace of God.","ref":"1 Peter 4:10 (KJV)"}},{"id":"temptation","name":"Temptation","cat":"living","overview":"Temptation is the pull toward sin. Scripture is honest that everyone faces it, but teaches that God never tempts anyone to evil and always provides a way of escape. Jesus was tempted in every way yet without sin, and He resisted the devil with the word of God.","refs":[{"ref":"Genesis 3:1–7","note":"The serpent tempts Eve"},{"ref":"Matthew 4:1–11","note":"Jesus tempted in the wilderness"},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 10:12–13","note":"God will make a way to escape"},{"ref":"James 1:12–15","note":"Every man is tempted when drawn away of his own lust"},{"ref":"Hebrews 4:14–16","note":"Tempted like as we are, yet without sin"},{"ref":"Matthew 26:41","note":"Watch and pray, that ye enter not into temptation"},{"ref":"Genesis 39:7–12","note":"Joseph flees from Potiphar's wife"}],"terms":[{"label":"Temptation","value":"An enticement to sin."},{"label":"Trial","value":"A testing of faith, which can strengthen it (James 1:3)."},{"label":"Lust","value":"Strong, sinful desire."},{"label":"Way of escape","value":"God's provided path out of temptation."},{"label":"Watchfulness","value":"Spiritual alertness."}],"questions":["How did the serpent twist God's word in Genesis 3?","How did Jesus answer each temptation in Matthew 4?","According to James 1:14–15, how does temptation grow into sin?","What can we learn from Joseph running from temptation?","What practical \"way of escape\" can you plan for a temptation you face?"],"quiz":[{"q":"How did Jesus answer the devil each time in Matthew 4?","a":"With Scripture: \"It is written.\""},{"q":"According to James 1:13, does God tempt anyone?","a":"No; God cannot be tempted with evil, nor does He tempt any man."},{"q":"What did Joseph leave behind when he fled?","a":"His garment (Genesis 39:12)."},{"q":"What did Jesus tell the disciples in Gethsemane to do so they would not enter temptation?","a":"Watch and pray (Matthew 26:41)."},{"q":"According to Hebrews 4:15, Jesus was tempted in all points like us yet what?","a":"Without sin."}],"verse":{"text":"There hath no temptation taken you but such as is common to man: but God is faithful, who will not suffer you to be tempted above that ye are able; but will with the temptation also make a way to escape, that ye may be able to bear it.","ref":"1 Corinthians 10:13 (KJV)"}},{"id":"anger","name":"Anger","cat":"living","overview":"Scripture distinguishes God's righteous anger against evil from human anger that is quick, selfish, and destructive. Believers are told to be slow to anger, not to let it lead to sin, and to resolve conflicts quickly. Jesus warned that anger in the heart is serious before God.","refs":[{"ref":"James 1:19–20","note":"Swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath"},{"ref":"Ephesians 4:26–27, 31–32","note":"Be ye angry, and sin not"},{"ref":"Proverbs 15:1","note":"A soft answer turneth away wrath"},{"ref":"Proverbs 29:11","note":"A fool uttereth all his mind"},{"ref":"Matthew 5:21–24","note":"Anger and reconciliation"},{"ref":"Genesis 4:3–8","note":"Cain's anger toward Abel"},{"ref":"Mark 3:1–5","note":"Jesus grieved and angered at hard hearts"}],"terms":[{"label":"Wrath","value":"Intense anger."},{"label":"Righteous anger","value":"Anger at evil and injustice, as God and Jesus displayed."},{"label":"Slow to anger","value":"Patient, not easily provoked."},{"label":"Reconciliation","value":"Restoring a broken relationship."},{"label":"Bitterness","value":"Lingering resentment (Ephesians 4:31)."}],"questions":["Why does James say human anger does not produce God's righteousness?","What does it mean to \"be angry and sin not\"?","How did God warn Cain about his anger?","What does Proverbs 15:1 suggest about how we answer others?","Is there a relationship you need to reconcile before you worship (Matthew 5:23–24)?"],"quiz":[{"q":"According to James 1:19, we should be swift to hear, slow to speak, and what?","a":"Slow to wrath."},{"q":"According to Proverbs 15:1, what turns away wrath?","a":"A soft answer."},{"q":"What did Paul say not to let the sun go down upon?","a":"Your wrath (Ephesians 4:26)."},{"q":"Who was the first person in the Bible whose anger led to murder?","a":"Cain (Genesis 4:8)."},{"q":"What should you do if your brother has something against you before offering your gift?","a":"First be reconciled to your brother (Matthew 5:24)."}],"verse":{"text":"Wherefore, my beloved brethren, let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath: For the wrath of man worketh not the righteousness of God.","ref":"James 1:19–20 (KJV)"}},{"id":"fear-anxiety","name":"Fear and Anxiety","cat":"living","overview":"\"Fear not\" is one of the most frequent commands in the Bible. Scripture acknowledges real dangers and worries but points people to God's presence, care, and promises. Believers are invited to cast their cares on Him in prayer and to replace fear with trust.","refs":[{"ref":"Isaiah 41:10","note":"Fear thou not; for I am with thee"},{"ref":"Psalm 23:4","note":"I will fear no evil: for thou art with me"},{"ref":"Psalm 56:3–4","note":"What time I am afraid, I will trust in thee"},{"ref":"Matthew 6:25–34","note":"Take no thought for your life"},{"ref":"Philippians 4:6–7","note":"Be careful for nothing"},{"ref":"1 Peter 5:6–7","note":"Casting all your care upon Him"},{"ref":"2 Timothy 1:7","note":"Not the spirit of fear, but of power, love, and a sound mind"}],"terms":[{"label":"Fear","value":"Alarm at danger; in Scripture distinct from reverent \"fear of the Lord\"."},{"label":"Anxiety","value":"Worry and unease about what might happen."},{"label":"Care","value":"In the KJV, anxious concern (1 Peter 5:7)."},{"label":"Trust","value":"Confident reliance on God."},{"label":"Sound mind","value":"Self-control and clear thinking (2 Timothy 1:7)."}],"questions":["Which \"fear not\" promise speaks to your situation right now?","What reasons does Jesus give in Matthew 6 for not worrying?","What practical steps does Philippians 4:6–8 give for anxiety?","How can you \"cast your care\" on God in a specific way today?","How can Christians support friends who struggle with anxiety, including encouraging wise professional help?"],"quiz":[{"q":"According to Isaiah 41:10, why should we not fear?","a":"Because God is with us and will strengthen, help, and uphold us."},{"q":"According to 1 Peter 5:7, why can we cast our care on God?","a":"Because He careth for you."},{"q":"What spirit has God not given us, according to 2 Timothy 1:7?","a":"The spirit of fear."},{"q":"Complete Psalm 56:3: \"What time I am afraid…\"","a":"\"…I will trust in thee.\""},{"q":"In Psalm 23:4, why does David fear no evil?","a":"\"For thou art with me.\""}],"verse":{"text":"Fear thou not; for I am with thee: be not dismayed; for I am thy God: I will strengthen thee; yea, I will help thee; yea, I will uphold thee with the right hand of my righteousness.","ref":"Isaiah 41:10 (KJV)"}},{"id":"hope","name":"Hope","cat":"living","overview":"Biblical hope is not wishful thinking but confident expectation based on God's promises. It is anchored in the resurrection of Jesus and looks forward to the renewal of all things. Hope sustains believers in suffering and makes them people who encourage others.","refs":[{"ref":"Romans 5:1–5","note":"Hope maketh not ashamed"},{"ref":"Romans 15:13","note":"The God of hope fill you with all joy and peace"},{"ref":"Hebrews 6:17–20","note":"Hope as an anchor of the soul"},{"ref":"1 Peter 1:3–5","note":"A lively hope by the resurrection of Jesus Christ"},{"ref":"Lamentations 3:21–26","note":"This I recall to mind, therefore have I hope"},{"ref":"Psalm 42","note":"Why art thou cast down, O my soul? Hope thou in God"},{"ref":"Jeremiah 29:11","note":"Thoughts of peace, to give you an expected end"}],"terms":[{"label":"Hope","value":"Confident expectation of what God has promised."},{"label":"Anchor","value":"Image of hope holding the soul steady (Hebrews 6:19)."},{"label":"Lively hope","value":"A living hope (1 Peter 1:3)."},{"label":"Expected end","value":"In Jeremiah 29:11 (KJV), a future and a hope."},{"label":"Despair","value":"The loss of hope."}],"questions":["How is biblical hope different from optimism?","What does the psalmist do with his discouragement in Psalm 42?","How does the resurrection give a \"lively hope\" (1 Peter 1:3)?","In context, to whom was Jeremiah 29:11 first spoken, and how does that shape how we apply it?","Who in your life needs encouragement in hope?"],"quiz":[{"q":"What image does Hebrews 6:19 use for hope?","a":"An anchor of the soul."},{"q":"According to 1 Peter 1:3, by what are believers begotten to a lively hope?","a":"The resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead."},{"q":"Psalm 42 compares longing for God to what animal?","a":"A hart (deer) panting after water brooks."},{"q":"According to Romans 15:13, God is the God of what?","a":"Hope."},{"q":"To whom did Jeremiah write the letter containing Jeremiah 29:11?","a":"The exiles carried away to Babylon (Jeremiah 29:1–4)."}],"verse":{"text":"Now the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace in believing, that ye may abound in hope, through the power of the Holy Ghost.","ref":"Romans 15:13 (KJV)"}},{"id":"gratitude","name":"Gratitude","cat":"living","overview":"Gratitude is thankful recognition of God's goodness and gifts. The Psalms overflow with thanksgiving, Jesus noticed when only one healed leper returned to give thanks, and Paul tells believers to give thanks in everything. Gratitude guards the heart against complaining and pride.","refs":[{"ref":"Psalm 100","note":"Enter into His gates with thanksgiving"},{"ref":"Psalm 103:1–5","note":"Forget not all His benefits"},{"ref":"Psalm 136","note":"His mercy endureth for ever"},{"ref":"Luke 17:11–19","note":"One of the ten lepers returns to give thanks"},{"ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:16–18","note":"In every thing give thanks"},{"ref":"Colossians 3:15–17","note":"Be ye thankful"},{"ref":"Philippians 4:6","note":"With thanksgiving let your requests be made known"}],"terms":[{"label":"Gratitude","value":"Thankfulness for what has been received."},{"label":"Thanksgiving","value":"Expressing thanks, especially to God."},{"label":"Benefits","value":"God's good gifts (Psalm 103:2)."},{"label":"Doxology","value":"A short hymn of praise."},{"label":"Murmuring","value":"Complaining (Philippians 2:14)."}],"questions":["Why do you think only one of the ten lepers returned?","What \"benefits\" does Psalm 103 list?","How can you give thanks \"in every thing\" without pretending hardship isn't real?","How does gratitude affect relationships?","List three things you are thankful for today."],"quiz":[{"q":"How many lepers did Jesus heal in Luke 17?","a":"Ten."},{"q":"What nationality was the leper who returned to give thanks?","a":"A Samaritan (Luke 17:16)."},{"q":"What refrain repeats in every verse of Psalm 136?","a":"\"For his mercy endureth for ever.\""},{"q":"According to 1 Thessalonians 5:18, in what should we give thanks?","a":"In every thing."},{"q":"According to Psalm 100:4, how should we enter God's gates?","a":"With thanksgiving."}],"verse":{"text":"In every thing give thanks: for this is the will of God in Christ Jesus concerning you.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:18 (KJV)"}},{"id":"integrity","name":"Integrity","cat":"living","overview":"Integrity means being whole and consistent: the same in private as in public, truthful in word and honest in deed. Scripture honors people like Job and Daniel, whose integrity held under pressure, and calls believers to let their yes be yes and their no be no.","refs":[{"ref":"Proverbs 10:9","note":"He that walketh uprightly walketh surely"},{"ref":"Proverbs 11:1–3","note":"The integrity of the upright shall guide them"},{"ref":"Job 2:3–10","note":"Job holds fast his integrity"},{"ref":"Psalm 15","note":"Who shall abide in thy tabernacle?"},{"ref":"Daniel 6:1–5","note":"No fault found in Daniel"},{"ref":"Matthew 5:33–37","note":"Let your communication be, Yea, yea; Nay, nay"},{"ref":"Luke 16:10","note":"Faithful in least, faithful in much"}],"terms":[{"label":"Integrity","value":"Moral wholeness; consistency of character."},{"label":"Upright","value":"Morally straight and honest."},{"label":"False balance","value":"Dishonest scales (Proverbs 11:1)."},{"label":"Blameless","value":"Without legitimate charge of wrongdoing."},{"label":"Oath","value":"A solemn promise invoking God as witness."}],"questions":["How did Job's integrity show itself in his suffering?","Why could Daniel's enemies find no fault in him?","What does Psalm 15 say marks a person who dwells with God?","Why does Jesus say a simple yes or no should be enough?","Where is integrity hardest for you, and what would help?"],"quiz":[{"q":"Who held fast his integrity even when his wife told him to curse God?","a":"Job (Job 2:9–10)."},{"q":"According to Proverbs 11:1, what is an abomination to the Lord?","a":"A false balance."},{"q":"What could Daniel's enemies find against him?","a":"Nothing, except concerning the law of his God (Daniel 6:5)."},{"q":"What did Jesus say our communication should be?","a":"\"Yea, yea; Nay, nay\" (Matthew 5:37)."},{"q":"According to Proverbs 10:9, how does the one who walks uprightly walk?","a":"Surely (securely)."}],"verse":{"text":"He that walketh uprightly walketh surely: but he that perverteth his ways shall be known.","ref":"Proverbs 10:9 (KJV)"}},{"id":"courage","name":"Courage","cat":"living","overview":"Courage in Scripture is not the absence of fear but faithful action in spite of it, grounded in God's presence. Joshua was told to be strong and courageous, Esther risked her life for her people, and the apostles spoke boldly about Jesus. The Holy Spirit gives believers boldness to do what is right.","refs":[{"ref":"Joshua 1:6–9","note":"Be strong and of a good courage"},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 31:6","note":"He will not fail thee, nor forsake thee"},{"ref":"1 Samuel 17:32–50","note":"David faces Goliath"},{"ref":"Esther 4:13–16","note":"If I perish, I perish"},{"ref":"Daniel 3:13–18","note":"The three Hebrews refuse to bow"},{"ref":"Acts 4:13, 29–31","note":"The apostles speak with boldness"},{"ref":"Psalm 27:1–3","note":"The Lord is my light; whom shall I fear?"}],"terms":[{"label":"Courage","value":"Strength to act rightly in the face of fear or danger."},{"label":"Boldness","value":"Confident freedom in speaking (Greek \"parrhesia\")."},{"label":"Valour","value":"Courage in battle."},{"label":"Steadfast","value":"Firmly fixed and unmoving."},{"label":"Martyr","value":"Literally \"witness\"; one who dies for the faith."}],"questions":["Where did Joshua's courage come from, according to Joshua 1:9?","What did Shadrach, Meshach, and Abed-nego say \"if not\" God delivered them?","What did the apostles pray for in Acts 4:29?","What situation requires courage from you right now?","How does David's past experience with God give him courage against Goliath?"],"quiz":[{"q":"How many times does the Lord tell Joshua to be strong and courageous in Joshua 1?","a":"Three times (verses 6, 7, and 9); the people repeat it in verse 18."},{"q":"What did Esther say before going to the king?","a":"\"If I perish, I perish\" (Esther 4:16)."},{"q":"What did the council notice about Peter and John in Acts 4:13?","a":"Their boldness, and that they had been with Jesus."},{"q":"With what did David kill Goliath?","a":"A sling and a stone."},{"q":"According to Psalm 27:1, the Lord is my light and what?","a":"My salvation."}],"verse":{"text":"Have not I commanded thee? Be strong and of a good courage; be not afraid, neither be thou dismayed: for the LORD thy God is with thee whithersoever thou goest.","ref":"Joshua 1:9 (KJV)"}},{"id":"suffering","name":"Suffering and Trials","cat":"living","overview":"The Bible does not hide suffering. Job, the psalms of lament, and the prophets wrestle honestly with pain, and Jesus Himself suffered. Scripture teaches that God is present in suffering, can use trials to refine faith, comforts the afflicted, and will one day wipe away every tear.","refs":[{"ref":"Job 1:13–22","note":"Job's losses and worship"},{"ref":"Psalm 13","note":"How long, O Lord?"},{"ref":"Romans 8:18–28","note":"The sufferings of this present time"},{"ref":"2 Corinthians 1:3–7","note":"The God of all comfort"},{"ref":"2 Corinthians 4:7–18","note":"Our light affliction works a far more exceeding glory"},{"ref":"James 1:2–4","note":"Count it all joy when you fall into divers temptations"},{"ref":"1 Peter 4:12–19","note":"Think it not strange concerning the fiery trial"}],"terms":[{"label":"Lament","value":"A prayer that pours out grief and complaint to God."},{"label":"Affliction","value":"Suffering or distress."},{"label":"Trial","value":"A test of faith."},{"label":"Comfort","value":"Strength and consolation given in sorrow."},{"label":"Theodicy","value":"The question of how a good God relates to evil and suffering."}],"questions":["What does Psalm 13 show about bringing honest pain to God?","How did Job first respond to his losses?","How does 2 Corinthians 1:4 give suffering a purpose for others?","What does Paul compare present suffering with in Romans 8:18?","How can you be present with someone who is suffering without offering easy answers?"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Job say after losing his children and possessions?","a":"\"The Lord gave, and the Lord hath taken away; blessed be the name of the Lord\" (Job 1:21)."},{"q":"Which psalm begins \"How long wilt thou forget me, O Lord?\"","a":"Psalm 13."},{"q":"According to 2 Corinthians 1:3, God is the God of all what?","a":"Comfort."},{"q":"What does Paul call his affliction in 2 Corinthians 4:17?","a":"Light affliction, which is but for a moment."},{"q":"According to 1 Peter 4:12, how should we view the fiery trial?","a":"Think it not strange, as though some strange thing happened."}],"verse":{"text":"For I reckon that the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory which shall be revealed in us.","ref":"Romans 8:18 (KJV)"}},{"id":"rest-sabbath","name":"Rest and Sabbath","cat":"living","overview":"God rested on the seventh day and gave Israel the Sabbath as a gift of rest and a sign of His covenant. Jesus called Himself Lord of the Sabbath and invited the weary to find rest in Him. Christians observe rest and worship in different ways, but Scripture consistently presents rest as a good gift that expresses trust in God.","refs":[{"ref":"Genesis 2:1–3","note":"God rested on the seventh day"},{"ref":"Exodus 20:8–11","note":"Remember the sabbath day"},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 5:12–15","note":"Remember that thou wast a servant in Egypt"},{"ref":"Psalm 127:1–2","note":"He giveth His beloved sleep"},{"ref":"Mark 2:23–28","note":"The sabbath was made for man"},{"ref":"Matthew 11:28–30","note":"Come unto me, and I will give you rest"},{"ref":"Hebrews 4:1–11","note":"There remaineth a rest to the people of God"}],"terms":[{"label":"Sabbath","value":"Hebrew \"cease, rest\"; the seventh day of rest."},{"label":"Lord's Day","value":"The first day of the week, when many Christians gather (Revelation 1:10)."},{"label":"Rest","value":"Ceasing from labor; also peace in God."},{"label":"Yoke","value":"A wooden frame joining animals for work; a picture of discipleship."},{"label":"Hallowed","value":"Made holy, set apart."}],"questions":["Why do you think God built rest into creation?","What reason does Deuteronomy 5:15 give for the Sabbath?","What did Jesus mean that the Sabbath was made for man?","What does Jesus' easy yoke offer to the weary?","What would healthy rhythms of rest look like in your week?"],"quiz":[{"q":"On which day did God rest?","a":"The seventh day (Genesis 2:2)."},{"q":"Which commandment begins \"Remember the sabbath day\"?","a":"The fourth commandment (Exodus 20:8)."},{"q":"According to Mark 2:28, who is Lord of the Sabbath?","a":"The Son of man."},{"q":"What does Jesus promise those who come to Him in Matthew 11:28?","a":"Rest."},{"q":"According to Psalm 127:2, what does God give His beloved?","a":"Sleep."}],"verse":{"text":"Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest.","ref":"Matthew 11:28 (KJV)"}},{"id":"speech","name":"Speech and the Tongue","cat":"living","overview":"Words have great power to build up or tear down. James compares the tongue to a small fire that can set a forest ablaze, and Proverbs is full of wisdom about speaking truthfully, gently, and at the right time. Believers are called to speak words that give grace to the hearer.","refs":[{"ref":"James 3:1–12","note":"Taming the tongue"},{"ref":"Proverbs 18:21","note":"Death and life are in the power of the tongue"},{"ref":"Proverbs 12:18–19","note":"The tongue of the wise is health"},{"ref":"Ephesians 4:29","note":"Let no corrupt communication proceed out of your mouth"},{"ref":"Colossians 4:6","note":"Let your speech be alway with grace"},{"ref":"Matthew 12:34–37","note":"Out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaketh"},{"ref":"Psalm 141:3","note":"Set a watch, O Lord, before my mouth"}],"terms":[{"label":"Edify","value":"To build up."},{"label":"Corrupt communication","value":"Unwholesome, harmful talk."},{"label":"Gossip","value":"Talking about others' private matters, often harmfully (KJV \"talebearer\")."},{"label":"Seasoned with salt","value":"Speech that is wise and appealing (Colossians 4:6)."},{"label":"Idle word","value":"A careless, useless word (Matthew 12:36)."}],"questions":["What images does James use for the tongue?","According to Jesus, where do our words come from?","What kind of words \"minister grace\" to hearers (Ephesians 4:29)?","Which of your speech habits would you like God to change?","How can you use words to build someone up today?"],"quiz":[{"q":"According to Proverbs 18:21, what is in the power of the tongue?","a":"Death and life."},{"q":"James compares the tongue to what small thing that kindles a great matter?","a":"A little fire."},{"q":"According to Matthew 12:34, out of what does the mouth speak?","a":"The abundance of the heart."},{"q":"How should our speech be seasoned, according to Colossians 4:6?","a":"With salt."},{"q":"What did the psalmist ask God to set before his mouth?","a":"A watch (a guard) (Psalm 141:3)."}],"verse":{"text":"Let no corrupt communication proceed out of your mouth, but that which is good to the use of edifying, that it may minister grace unto the hearers.","ref":"Ephesians 4:29 (KJV)"}},{"id":"evangelism","name":"Sharing Your Faith","cat":"living","overview":"Evangelism means sharing the good news of Jesus. The early church spoke of what they had seen and heard, and every believer is called to be ready to give a reason for their hope with gentleness and respect. Scripture shows many ways of witness, from public preaching to personal conversation.","refs":[{"ref":"Romans 1:16","note":"I am not ashamed of the gospel"},{"ref":"Romans 10:13–15","note":"How shall they hear without a preacher?"},{"ref":"1 Peter 3:15","note":"Be ready always to give an answer"},{"ref":"John 4:28–42","note":"The Samaritan woman tells her town"},{"ref":"Acts 8:26–39","note":"Philip and the Ethiopian eunuch"},{"ref":"2 Corinthians 5:17–21","note":"The ministry of reconciliation; ambassadors for Christ"},{"ref":"Colossians 4:5–6","note":"Walk in wisdom toward them that are without"}],"terms":[{"label":"Evangelism","value":"Sharing the gospel (good news) of Jesus."},{"label":"Gospel","value":"\"Good news\" of salvation through Christ."},{"label":"Witness","value":"One who tells what they have seen and experienced."},{"label":"Ambassador","value":"An official representative (2 Corinthians 5:20)."},{"label":"Apologetics","value":"Giving a reasoned defense of the faith (from Greek \"apologia,\" 1 Peter 3:15)."}],"questions":["What did the Samaritan woman say to her town, and why was it effective?","How did Philip begin his conversation with the Ethiopian?","What does it mean to give an answer \"with meekness and fear\"?","What is your own story of how you came to faith?","Who is one person you could pray for and talk with about Jesus?"],"quiz":[{"q":"Why was Paul not ashamed of the gospel (Romans 1:16)?","a":"Because it is the power of God unto salvation to everyone that believes."},{"q":"What was the Ethiopian reading when Philip met him?","a":"The prophet Isaiah (Acts 8:28–32)."},{"q":"What does 2 Corinthians 5:20 call believers?","a":"Ambassadors for Christ."},{"q":"According to 1 Peter 3:15, what should we be ready to give?","a":"An answer (a reason) for the hope that is in us."},{"q":"What did the Samaritan woman leave at the well?","a":"Her waterpot (John 4:28)."}],"verse":{"text":"For I am not ashamed of the gospel of Christ: for it is the power of God unto salvation to every one that believeth; to the Jew first, and also to the Greek.","ref":"Romans 1:16 (KJV)"}},{"id":"perseverance","name":"Perseverance","cat":"living","overview":"Perseverance is continuing faithfully to the end. Scripture compares the Christian life to a race run with endurance, looking to Jesus. Trials develop steadfastness, and God promises to keep His people as they hold fast to Him.","refs":[{"ref":"Hebrews 12:1–3","note":"Run with patience the race set before us"},{"ref":"Galatians 6:9","note":"Be not weary in well doing"},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 9:24–27","note":"So run, that ye may obtain"},{"ref":"Philippians 3:12–14","note":"I press toward the mark"},{"ref":"James 1:12","note":"Blessed is the man that endureth temptation"},{"ref":"2 Timothy 4:6–8","note":"I have finished my course, I have kept the faith"},{"ref":"Philippians 1:6","note":"He which hath begun a good work in you will perform it"}],"terms":[{"label":"Perseverance","value":"Steady persistence in faith and good works."},{"label":"Endurance","value":"The ability to bear up under difficulty."},{"label":"Crown of life","value":"The reward promised to those who endure (James 1:12)."},{"label":"Course","value":"A race or path of life (2 Timothy 4:7)."},{"label":"Cloud of witnesses","value":"The faithful of Hebrews 11 who surround us (Hebrews 12:1)."}],"questions":["What weights or sins slow you down in the Christian race?","How does looking to Jesus help us endure (Hebrews 12:2)?","What did Paul mean by \"forgetting those things which are behind\"?","How does Philippians 1:6 encourage those who feel they are failing?","Who has modeled faithful perseverance for you?"],"quiz":[{"q":"According to Hebrews 12:2, to whom do we look as we run?","a":"Jesus, the author and finisher of our faith."},{"q":"According to Galatians 6:9, when shall we reap?","a":"In due season, if we faint not."},{"q":"What did Paul say he had done in 2 Timothy 4:7?","a":"Fought a good fight, finished his course, kept the faith."},{"q":"What does James 1:12 promise the one who endures?","a":"The crown of life."},{"q":"Toward what did Paul press in Philippians 3:14?","a":"The mark for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus."}],"verse":{"text":"And let us not be weary in well doing: for in due season we shall reap, if we faint not.","ref":"Galatians 6:9 (KJV)"}}],"commentary":{"faith":[{"passage":"Hebrews 11:1–3","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/hebrews/11.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/hebrews/11.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Faith demonstrates to the eye of the mind the reality of those things that cannot be discerned by the eye of the body."},"jfb":{"kind":"Summary","text":"JFB says verse 1 is not a full definition of faith but a description of what faith does for us, given to encourage readers to persevere."}},{"passage":"James 2:14–26","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/james/2.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/james/2.htm","mh":{"kind":"Summary","text":"Henry takes up the classic question of how to reconcile Paul (justified by faith) and James (not by faith only), arguing the two agree: James warns against resting on a bare profession of faith that never changes one's life."},"jfb":{"kind":"Summary","text":"Citing Bengel, JFB explains that if the works a living faith produces are absent, it shows that the faith someone boasts of does not really exist; it is dead."}}],"prayer":[{"passage":"Matthew 6:9–15","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/matthew/6.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/matthew/6.htm","mh":{"kind":"Summary","text":"Henry sees the Lord's Prayer as Jesus helping our weakness by putting words in our mouths: a pattern for the matter and method of prayer that may be used as a form, though we are not tied to it alone."},"jfb":{"kind":"Summary","text":"JFB argues the prayer was given not only as a model but as an actual prayer to be prayed, showing how much real prayer fits in the fewest words."}},{"passage":"Philippians 4:6–7","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/philippians/4.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/philippians/4.htm","mh":{"kind":"Summary","text":"Henry distinguishes a good care of diligence (wise planning) from a care of distrust that perplexes the mind, and offers constant prayer as the remedy for anxious care."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Care and prayer are as mutually opposed as fire and water [Bengel]."}}],"forgiveness":[{"passage":"Matthew 18:21–35","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/matthew/18.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/matthew/18.htm","mh":{"kind":"Summary","text":"Henry observes that Peter already assumed he must forgive, but thought seven times in a lifetime was generous; the teaching concerns personal wrongs that are in our power to forgive."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"you are never to come to the point of refusing forgiveness sincerely asked."}},{"passage":"Genesis 50:15–21","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/genesis/50.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/genesis/50.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"God often brings good out of evil, and promotes the designs of his providence even by the sins of men"},"jfb":{"kind":"Summary","text":"JFB says Joseph, deeply moved, gave his brothers the strongest assurances of forgiveness, showing his own godly character and serving as a picture of the Saviour."}}],"humility":[{"passage":"Philippians 2:1–4","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/philippians/2.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/philippians/2.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"He presses them largely to like-mindedness and lowly-mindedness, in conformity to the example of the Lord Jesus, the great pattern of humility and love."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The man \"lowly of mind\" as to his spiritual life is independent of men, and free from all slavish feeling, while sensible of his continual dependence on God."}},{"passage":"Luke 14:7–14","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/luke/14.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/luke/14.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"We see in other instances that a man’s pride will bring him low, but honour shall uphold the humble in spirit, and before honour is humility."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Jesus certainly did not mean us to dispense with the duties of ordinary fellowship, but, remitting these to their proper place, inculcates what is better [Bengel]."}}],"obedience":[{"passage":"1 Samuel 15:22–23","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/1_samuel/15.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/1_samuel/15.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Now here we are plainly told that humble, sincere, and conscientious obedience to the will of God, is more pleasing and acceptable to him than all burnt-offerings and sacrifices."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Samuel saw the real state of the case, and in discharge of the commission he had received before setting out, proceeded to denounce his conduct as characterized by pride, rebellion, and obstinate disobedience."}},{"passage":"James 1:22–27","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/james/1.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/james/1.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"May God by his grace keep both our hearts and lives clean from the love of the world, and from the temptations of wicked worldly men."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"As a man may behold his natural face in a mirror, so the hearer may perceive his moral visage in God's Word."}}],"wisdom":[{"passage":"Proverbs 3:1–6","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/proverbs/3.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/proverbs/3.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"In all our conduct we must be diffident of our own judgment, and confident of God’s wisdom, power, and goodness, and therefore must follow Providence and not force it."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"God must be feared, honored, and trusted, and filial submission, under chastisement, exhibited."}},{"passage":"James 3:13–18","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/james/3.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/james/3.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"We have some account of true wisdom, with the distinguishing marks and fruits of it: Who is a wise man, and endued with knowledge among you?"},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"\"The peaceable fruit of righteousness.\" He says \"righteousness\"; because it is itself the true wisdom."}}],"contentment":[{"passage":"Philippians 4:10–19","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/philippians/4.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/philippians/4.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"It is an excellent thing to see those to whom God has abounded in the gifts of his grace abounding in grateful returns to his people and ministers, according to their own ability and their necessity: You sent once and again."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Greek, \"I have strength for all things\"; not merely \"how to be abased and how to abound.\" After special instances he declares his universal power—how triumphantly, yet how humbly! [Meyer]."}},{"passage":"1 Timothy 6:6–12","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/1_timothy/6.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/1_timothy/6.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Godliness with contentment, that is, Christian contentment (content must come from principles of godliness) is great gain; it is all the wealth in the world."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Greek, \"covering\"; according to some including a roof to cover us, that is, a dwelling, as well as clothing."}}],"generosity":[{"passage":"2 Corinthians 9:6–11","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/2_corinthians/9.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/2_corinthians/9.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"We have no reason to distrust the goodness of God, and surely we have no reason to question his power; he is able to make all grace abound towards us, and abound in us; to give a large increase of spiritual and temporal good things."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"\"The reaping shall correspond to the proportions and spirit of the sowing\" [Bengel]."}},{"passage":"Mark 12:41–44","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/mark/12.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/luke/21.htm","jfbOn":"Luke 21:1–4","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"It is very good for us to straiten and deny ourselves, that we may be able to give the more to the poor; to deny ourselves not only superfluities, but even conveniences, for the sake of charity."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"And he said—\"to His disciples,\" whom He \"called to Him\" (Mr 12:43), to teach from it a great future lesson."}}],"stewardship":[{"passage":"Luke 16:10–13","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/luke/16.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/luke/16.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"We have not made a due improvement of what God has entrusted us with in this world, but have perverted his purpose; and, that we may not be for this judged of our Lord, it concerns us to judge ourselves."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Here all we have is on trust as stewards, who have an account to render."}},{"passage":"1 Corinthians 4:1–5","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/1_corinthians/4.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/1_corinthians/4.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The Lord Jesus Christ will manifest the counsels of the heart, of all hearts."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Though here below, in the case of stewards, inquiry is made, that one man be found (that is, proved to be) faithful; yet God's steward awaits no such judgment of man, in man's day, but the Lord's judgment in His great day."}}],"temptation":[{"passage":"James 1:12–15","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/james/1.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/james/1.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Our enduring temptations must be from a principle of love to God and to our Lord Jesus Christ, otherwise we are not interested in this promise: The Lord hath promised to those that love him."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Then the particular sin (so the Greek implies), \"when it is completed, brings forth death,\" with which it was all along pregnant [Alford]."}},{"passage":"Matthew 4:1–11","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/matthew/4.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/matthew/4.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"This method we must take when at any time we are tempted to sin; resist and repel the temptation with, It is written."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The temptation evidently embraced two stages: the one continuing throughout the forty days' fast; the other, at the conclusion of that period."}}],"anger":[{"passage":"Ephesians 4:25–32","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/ephesians/4.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/ephesians/4.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Corrupt passions of bitterness, wrath, anger, clamour, evil-speaking, and malice, grieve the Holy Spirit."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Passion is sinful (derived from \"passio,\" suffering: implying that amidst seeming energy, a man is really passive, the slave of his anger, instead of ruling it)."}},{"passage":"Genesis 4:3–8","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/genesis/4.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/genesis/4.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"God is here reasoning with Cain, to convince him of the sin and folly of his anger and discontent, and to bring him into a good temper again, that further mischief might be prevented."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"If thou doest well (that is, wert innocent and sinless) a thank offering would have been accepted as a token of thy dependence as a creature."}}],"fear-anxiety":[{"passage":"Isaiah 41:8–13","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/isaiah/41.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/isaiah/41.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The scope of these verses is to silence the fears, and encourage the faith, of the servants of God in their distresses."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Contrast between the idolatrous nations whom God will destroy by Cyrus, and Israel whom God will deliver by the same man for their forefathers' sake."}},{"passage":"1 Peter 5:6–11","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/1_peter/5.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/1_peter/5.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The consideration of what others suffer is proper to encourage us to bear our own share in any affliction: The same afflictions are accomplished in your brethren."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Be sober … vigilant—\"Care,\" that is, anxiety, will intoxicate the soul; therefore be sober, that is, self-restrained."}}],"hope":[{"passage":"Romans 5:1–5","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/romans/5.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/romans/5.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Those, and those only, that have access by faith into the grace of God now may hope for the glory of God hereafter."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"And hope maketh not ashamed—putteth not to shame, as empty hopes do."}},{"passage":"Psalm 42:1–5","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/psalms/42.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/psalms/42.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"David’s believing expectation of this kept him from sinking, nay, it kept him from drooping; his harp was a palliative cure of Saul’s melancholy, but his hope was an effectual cure of his own."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Hence he chides his despondent soul, assuring himself of a time of joy."}}],"gratitude":[{"passage":"Luke 17:11–19","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/luke/17.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/luke/17.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The rest were made whole by the power of Christ, in compassion to their distress, and in answer to their prayer; but he was made whole by his faith, by which Christ saw him distinguished from the rest."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"faith made thee whole—not as the others, merely in body, but in that higher spiritual sense with which His constant language has so familiarized us."}},{"passage":"Psalm 100","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/psalms/100.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/psalms/100.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"We must not only comfort ourselves, but glorify God, with our joy, and let him have the praise of that which we have the pleasure of."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The reason: God's eternal mercy and truth (Ps 25:8; 89:7)."}}],"integrity":[{"passage":"Proverbs 11:1–3","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/proverbs/11.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/proverbs/11.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The integrity of an honest man will itself be his guide in the way of duty and the way of safety."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Self-conceit is unteachable; the humble grow wise (compare Pr 16:18; 18:12)."}},{"passage":"Daniel 6:1–5","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/daniel/6.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/daniel/6.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"This is recorded for an example to all that are in places of public trust to approve themselves both careful and conscientious, that they may be free, not only from fault, but from error, not only from crime, but from mistake."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"No doubt Cyrus acted under Darius, as in the capture of Babylon; so that Daniel rightly attributes the appointment to Darius."}}],"courage":[{"passage":"Joshua 1:5–9","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhc/joshua/1.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/joshua/1.htm","mhEd":"Concise","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"When we are in the way of duty, we have reason to be strong and very bold."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"What life and encouragement must have been imparted to Joshua by the assurance that his people, who had been overwhelmed with fear of that gigantic race, were to possess \"all the land of the Hittites\"!"}},{"passage":"Daniel 3:13–18","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/daniel/3.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/daniel/3.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The wrath of a king is as the roaring of a lion; so was the wrath of this king; and yet, when he was in such a heat, these three men were brought before him, and appeared with an undaunted courage, and unshaken constancy."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"\"Whether our God deliver us, as He is able, or do not, we will not serve thy gods.\" Their service of God is not mercenary in its motive."}}],"suffering":[{"passage":"2 Corinthians 4:7–18","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/2_corinthians/4.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/2_corinthians/4.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The prospect of eternal life and happiness kept them from fainting, and was a mighty support and comfort."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The fact of a dying, corpse-like body being sustained amidst such trials, manifests that \"the (resurrection) life also,\" as well as the dying, \"of Jesus,\" exerts its power in us."}},{"passage":"1 Peter 4:12–19","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/1_peter/4.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/1_peter/4.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Those who rejoice in their sufferings for Christ shall eternally triumph and rejoice with him in glory."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"If we will not bear suffering for Christ now, we must bear eternal sufferings hereafter."}}],"rest-sabbath":[{"passage":"Matthew 11:28–30","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/matthew/11.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/matthew/11.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Christ is our Noah, whose name signifies rest, for this same shall give us rest."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"That rest which the soul experiences when once safe under Christ's wing makes all yokes easy, all burdens light."}},{"passage":"Mark 2:23–28","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/mark/2.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/matthew/12.htm","jfbOn":"Matthew 12:1–8","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Man was not made for the sabbath, for he was made a day before the sabbath was instituted."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"How he entered into the house of God, and did eat the showbread, which was not lawful for him to eat, neither for them which were with him, but only for the priests?—No example could be more apposite than this."}}],"speech":[{"passage":"James 3:1–12","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/james/3.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/james/3.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"We are taught to govern our tongue so as to prove ourselves perfect and upright men, and such as have an entire government over ourselves: If any man offend not in word, the same is a perfect man, and able also to bridle the whole body."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Grace can make the same mouth that \"sent forth the bitter\" once, send forth the sweet for the time to come: as the wood (typical of Christ's cross) changed Marah's bitter water into sweet."}},{"passage":"Matthew 12:33–37","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/matthew/12.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/matthew/12.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"He seems to turn from the Pharisees to the people, from disputing to instructing; and from the sin of the Pharisees he warns the people concerning three sorts of tongue-sins; for others’ harms are admonitions to us."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The word \"putteth\" indicates the spontaneity of what comes from the heart; for it is out of the abundance of the heart that the mouth speaketh."}}],"evangelism":[{"passage":"Acts 8:26–39","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/acts/8.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/acts/8.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Philip takes this fair occasion given him to open to him the great mystery of the gospel concerning Jesus Christ, and him crucified."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"One cannot but wonder that this, of all predictions of Messiah's sufferings in the Old Testament the most striking, should have been that which the eunuch was reading before Philip joined him."}},{"passage":"1 Peter 3:13–17","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/1_peter/3.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/1_peter/3.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"There may be a good reason given for religion; it is not a fancy but a rational scheme revealed from heaven, suited to all the necessities of miserable sinners, and centering entirely in the glory of God through Jesus Christ."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Having a good conscience—the secret spring of readiness to give account of our hope."}}],"perseverance":[{"passage":"Hebrews 12:1–3","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/hebrews/12.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/hebrews/12.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"There will be need of patience to encounter the difficulties that lie in our way, of perseverance to resist all temptations to desist or turn aside."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Going before us as the Originator of our faith, and the Leader whose matchless example we are to follow always."}},{"passage":"Philippians 3:12–16","mhUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/mhcw/philippians/3.htm","jfbUrl":"https://biblehub.com/commentaries/jfb/philippians/3.htm","mh":{"kind":"Quote","text":"Heaven is the prize of the high calling; to brabeion—the prize we fight for, and run for, and wrestle for, what we aim at in all we do, and what will reward all our pains."},"jfb":{"kind":"Quote","text":"The Christian is always humbled by the contrast between what he is and what he desires to be."}}]}});
